Bash Notes
Indexed Arrays
------------------


when accessing an array:
* stringifies
@ iterates

Double "" quotes and @ sign is how to properly loop over an array in Bash.

"${#array[@]}" to get the length of an array

Example:

array=(foo bar baz)
declare -p array
- delcare -a array=([0]="foo" [1]="bar" [2]="baz")
array=([0]="foo" [1]="bar" [2]="baz" [45]="buddy")
declare -p array
- declare -a array([0]="foo" [1]="bar" [2]="baz" [45]="buddy")
echo "${array[45]}"
- buddy
echo "${#array[@]}"
- 4


To get the length of the string in an array it would be:
"${#array[45]}"
In this case "buddy" is at index 45 and would return 5

You can also do this for normal variables to get the length of a string:
var="hello world"
echo ${#var}
and the result would be 11




